---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import FlowDiagram from '../components/FlowDiagram.astro';
import Code from '../components/Code.astro';
const source = 'https://github.com/paddo-tech/tether-cli/blob/main/';
---

<BaseLayout title="Security — Tether CLI" description="See how Tether encrypts dotfiles, checks synced packages before it installs them, and decides which machines to trust.">
  <main id="main">
    <section class="page-hero wrap"><div><p class="eyebrow"><Icon name="lock" size={15} /> SECURITY, IN THE OPEN</p><h1>Your files.<br /><em>Your keys.</em></h1><p class="lede">Tether encrypts dotfiles on your machine before they reach Git. It checks synced packages before it installs them. You control the repository, the passphrase and the machines you trust.</p><a class="text-link" href={`${source}src/security/encryption.rs`}>Read the encryption code <span>↗</span></a></div><div class="security-summary"><span class="summary-icon"><Icon name="lock" size={36} /></span><h2>Encrypted before<br />they leave.</h2><dl><div><dt>Personal dotfiles</dt><dd>AES-256-GCM</dd></div><div><dt>Encryption key</dt><dd>Protected with age</dd></div><div><dt>Storage</dt><dd>Your Git repository</dd></div><div><dt>Synced packages</dt><dd>Checked before install</dd></div><div><dt>Machine records</dt><dd>Signed with SSH keys</dd></div></dl></div></section>
    <section class="content-section wrap" id="encryption"><div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">FOLLOW A FILE</p><h2>Plaintext here.<br />Encrypted in Git.</h2><p>Your local shell reads your dotfiles normally. Tether stores an encrypted copy in your sync repository.</p></div><FlowDiagram label="PERSONAL DOTFILE ENCRYPTION" caption="Encryption is enabled by default. Package manifests remain readable in Git." steps={[
      { icon: 'file', title: 'Local dotfile', detail: '.zshrc · Readable on your machine' },
      { icon: 'lock', title: 'Encrypt locally', detail: 'AES-256-GCM · Fresh nonce' },
      { icon: 'git', title: 'Sync through Git', detail: '.zshrc.enc · Encrypted contents' },
    ]} /><div class="detail-columns"><article><h3>Authenticated encryption.</h3><p>Tether uses a 256-bit key and a new random 96-bit nonce for each encryption operation.</p><p>The authentication tag detects changes to the encrypted contents.</p></article><article><h3>A portable encrypted key.</h3><p>Tether protects your encryption key with age and your passphrase. The encrypted key travels with your Git repo.</p><p>Your passphrase does not go into the repository.</p></article><article><h3>Local files stay readable.</h3><p>Your shell needs plaintext dotfiles. Protect your devices and local accounts, including the cached encryption key.</p><p>Encryption in Git does not protect an unlocked machine.</p></article></div></section>
    <section class="split-section wrap" id="key-management"><div class="section-copy"><p class="eyebrow">ONE PASSPHRASE. YOUR MACHINES.</p><h2>Unlock your setup.<br /><em>Keep control.</em></h2><p>The first machine creates an encryption key. Other machines retrieve the encrypted key from your repo.</p><p>Run <code>tether unlock</code> to decrypt and cache it locally. Run <code>tether lock</code> to clear the cache.</p><p>Locking clears the key cache. Your local plaintext dotfiles remain on disk.</p><Code code={'tether unlock\ntether lock'} /></div><FlowDiagram label="ON YOUR NEXT MACHINE" caption="The decrypted key cache stays local. Tether does not sync it to Git." steps={[
      { icon: 'git', title: 'Get the key', detail: 'encryption.key.age from your repo' },
      { icon: 'key', title: 'Enter your passphrase', detail: 'Decrypt the key with age' },
      { icon: 'file', title: 'Read your dotfiles', detail: 'Decrypt and apply locally' },
    ]} /></section>
    <section class="team-callout wrap" id="supply-chain"><Icon name="shield" size={31} /><div><p class="eyebrow">SUPPLY-CHAIN SECURITY</p><h2>Synced packages.<br />Checked first.</h2><p>Tether checks each synced package before it installs it. Machines sign their records, and you choose which machines to trust.</p></div><a class="button secondary" href="/supply-chain">See the package checks <Icon name="arrow" size={18} /></a></section>
    <section class="content-section wrap" id="secret-detection"><div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">CHECK BEFORE YOU SYNC</p><h2>Spot credentials<br />in your configs.</h2><p>Tether scans files for potential secrets. Review the warnings before you continue syncing.</p></div><div class="security-grid"><article class="surface-panel"><Icon name="file" size={27} /><h3>What the scanner looks for.</h3><ul class="check-list"><li>AWS access keys and GitHub tokens</li><li>API keys and bearer tokens</li><li>SSH and RSA private keys</li><li>Passwords and database credentials</li><li>High-entropy strings</li></ul></article><article class="surface-panel"><Icon name="lock" size={27} /><h3>What the scanner cannot promise.</h3><p>Pattern matching can miss secrets or flag harmless text. Review your configuration and choose what you sync.</p><p>Use a private repository. Package manifests and repository metadata remain visible to people with repository access.</p><a class="text-link" href={`${source}src/security/secrets.rs`}>Review scanning patterns <span>↗</span></a></article></div></section>
    <section class="content-section wrap" id="threat-model"><div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">KNOW THE BOUNDARIES</p><h2>Understand the protection.</h2></div><div class="detail-columns"><article><h3>Encryption protects stored contents.</h3><p>Repository access alone does not reveal encrypted dotfile contents without the key or passphrase.</p><p>AES-GCM detects tampering with encrypted data. Git transport security depends on your SSH or HTTPS configuration.</p></article><article><h3>Your repo can change, too.</h3><p>Anyone with push access can edit manifests and machine records. Signed records and your local trust list limit what a sync installs from those changes. See <a href="/supply-chain">supply-chain security</a>.</p><p>Protect each machine’s <code>~/.tether/signing_key</code>. Anyone with that key can sign records as that machine.</p></article><article><h3>Your device needs protection, too.</h3><p>Malware and compromised local accounts can access plaintext files or cached keys.</p><p>Use device encryption, a strong passphrase, and two-factor authentication for your Git host. Review secret warnings before syncing.</p></article></div></section>
    <section class="source-section wrap"><div><p class="eyebrow">VERIFY THE DETAILS</p><h2>The code is open.</h2><p>Read the implementation and its tests.</p></div><div class="source-links"><a href={`${source}src/security/encryption.rs`}><span>File encryption<small>encryption.rs</small></span><span>↗</span></a><a href={`${source}src/security/keychain.rs`}><span>Key management<small>keychain.rs</small></span><span>↗</span></a><a href={`${source}src/security/secrets.rs`}><span>Secret scanning<small>secrets.rs</small></span><span>↗</span></a><a href={`${source}Cargo.toml`}><span>Dependencies<small>Cargo.toml</small></span><span>↗</span></a></div></section>
    <section class="report-section wrap"><Icon name="lock" size={25} /><div><h2>Report a security issue.</h2><p>Email <a href="mailto:security@tether-cli.com">security@tether-cli.com</a> with the affected version, reproduction steps, and potential impact.</p><p>Keep vulnerability details out of public issues until a fix is available.</p></div></section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
